# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss Keeping the EFI partition too
The archive was removed afterwards:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-boot-manifest --esp-archive /var/tmp/onyx-esp.tar >/dev/null
capturing boot manifest for onyx
ESP archived: /var/tmp/onyx-esp.tar (389M)
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Pp
It is not small: most of that was a 244 MB initrd and two 65 MB
ZFSBootMenu images.
.Ss A pool that is not there
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-boot-manifest --pool nosuch
FATAL: no pool named nosuch on this host
# (exited 2)
.Ed
.Ss For another machine
Not run for this page; from the backup host, before the machine is lost:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-boot-manifest --host root@fiend --esp-archive fiend-esp.tar >fiend.json
# prints "capturing boot manifest for fiend" and the archive's size on
# standard error
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr jq 1 ,
.Xr ssh 1 ,
.Xr kldload-restore-machine 8 ,
.Xr syncoid 8
.Sh HISTORY
Written after fiend was restored on 2026-09-09: the pool was perfect and
the machine did not boot, and every fact above had to be found again by
looking.
.Pp
On 2026-09-30, writing this page: a pool that did not exist gave a
manifest of empty fields and exit 0; the EFI size was recorded as
.Xr findmnt 8
prints it, which a restore could hand to
.Xr sgdisk 8
as a decimal that it silently truncates; and a 389 MB archive was reported
as 1.0K.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
Every fact other than the pool's existence is read without checking
whether the read worked: a field that could not be read is empty, not an
error.
.Pp
The EFI archive holds whatever is on the partition, and is written with
the default file mode.
.Pp
It does not capture Secure Boot keys, only their number; re-enrolling
needs someone at the console.
